course: dictionary
lesson: 12761
title: Understanding the word dyslexic
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dyslexic is an adjective. It describes a person who has dyslexia, which is a difference in the way the brain handles words. «Maya is dyslexic, so reading a long page takes her more time.» The word dyslexic tells us what kind of difference Maya has.
explain: Dyslexia can make reading, spelling and sometimes writing harder. It has nothing to do with how clever someone is. «Sam is dyslexic, and he is also the best at maths in our class.» Both things can be true at the same time.
explain: Context helps you here too. «Our school gives dyslexic pupils extra time in tests.» The clues extra time and tests show that the help is for pupils who find words harder, not for pupils who are lazy. Many people say «a person with dyslexia», which puts the person first.
fact: Dyslexic is an adjective that describes someone who has dyslexia.
fact: Dyslexia is a difference in how the brain handles words.
fact: Dyslexia can make reading, spelling and sometimes writing harder, but it has nothing to do with intelligence.
fact: «Dyslexic-friendly font» means a letter style designed to be easier to read for people with dyslexia.
fact: Struggling to read is not being lazy or slow.
exercise: «Tom is dyslexic, so reading takes him longer.» What does dyslexic mean? | Reads faster than everyone else | Is very lazy about homework | Cannot learn anything at all | *Has a difference in how the brain handles words
exercise: «Our school gives dyslexic pupils extra time in tests.» Why do they get extra time? | *Because dyslexia can make reading and spelling harder | Because they are the cleverest pupils in the school | Because they are late for lessons every day | As a punishment
exercise: Jay says, «Dyslexic people are not clever.» Is he right? | Only sometimes, on sunny days | Yes, dyslexic people always find thinking hard | Yes, because dyslexic people cannot read at all | *No, dyslexia has nothing to do with how clever someone is
test: Which phrase puts the person first and is respectful? | the dyslexic kid who is slow | dyslexics who can't read | *a person with dyslexia | a dyslexic idiot
test: What does the idea «struggling to read is not being lazy» teach us? | *Kindness: a reading struggle is not a sign of laziness | That slow readers should try harder to be lazy | That spelling does not matter at all | That reading is easy for everyone
exam: «Dyslexic-friendly font» means a font that is | louder when printed | *easier to read for people with dyslexia | only used for maths books | written in a secret code
